            <ArticleTitle>Coronary heart disease risk factors among blood donors in northwest Ethiopia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>495-500</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To establish reference blood levels of total blood cholesterol (TC), lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C &amp; HDL-C) and triglyceride (TG) and determine the prevalence of hypercholesterolaemia and other coronary heart disease risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The blood bank at the Gondar College of Medical Sciences Hospital--a teaching and referral hospital in northwest Ethiopia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Adult, healthy men who who voluntarily donated blood between March 1996 and July 1997.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Mean, median and ranges of TC, LDL-C, HDL-C and TG; prevalence rates of hypercholesterolaemia, overweight, smoking and elevated blood pressures. Effect of age, BMI, residence, monthly income on blood lipid levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 501 donors were investigated and of these 316 (63%) were urban residents. The mean (SD) TC, LDL-C, HDL-C and TG levels in urban donors were 162.5 (35.1), 93.8 (33.9), 47.1 (8.1) and 108.1 (52.5); and in donors from rural area were 158.5 (30), 91.0 (30), 47.4 (7.4), and 98.6 (55.3), respectively; without significant difference between them. However, urban residency was significantly associated (B = 7.2, 95% CI = 1.5-12.9) with the total cholesterol level. The prevalence of hypercholesterolaemia (TC &gt; 200 mg) was 10.4% in urban donors and 8.1% in rural donors (chi 2 = 0.75, p = 0.39). The prevalence rates of smoking, high blood pressure and overweight in donors from urban and rural areas were, 14% versus 6%, 14% versus 14% and 1.6% versus 1.1%, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The prevalence of hypercholesterolaemia and other coronary heart disease risk factors are low in both urban and rural donors in northwest Ethiopia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
